ted for similar works worldwide (FWCI 1 = world average).","Above the world average: 0%–9% of the works per year have an FWCI above 1, most in 2016 (9%).","Not cited: on average 43% of these works have FWCI 0, meaning no citations within their four-year window. That is why many boxes start at 0.","Mean versus median: the mean is above the median in 2 of 2 years, because a few highly cited works pull the average up. The widest gap is 2016: mean 0.34, median 0.13, top work 3.18. The median is the better guide to a typical work.","Still counting: works from 2023–2026 have not completed their four-year citation window, so their values will rise. Do not compare them with older years yet.","Small base: the values for 2013–2015, 2018–2022 and 2024–2026 are based on only 10–29 works (lighter box or hollow point), so read them with care.","Few works: 2012 has fewer than 10 works (2012: 8), too few for a box. Each of those works is drawn as a dot."],"citations":["Typical work: works published 2015–2023 have a median of 1–3 citations each; the highest median (3) is in 2016.","Most cited works: the top 10% of works published in 2017 have at least 13.4 citations each, the highest P90 in the chart.","Not yet cited: more than half of the works published 2025–2026 have no citations so far. This is expected for recent works and is not a sign of low quality.","Mean versus median: the mean is above the median in 6 of 6 years, because a few highly cited works pull the average up. The widest gap is 2016: mean 5.5, median 3, top work 37. The median is the better guide to a typical work.","Recent years: works from 2024–2026 have had less than three years to be cited, so their values will rise.","Small base: the values for 2013–2014, 2019–2021 and 2024–2026 are based on only 10–29 works (lighter box or hollow point), so read them with care.","Few works: 2012 has fewer than 10 works (2012: 9), too few for a band. Each of those works is drawn as a dot."]}}}